---
// Lesson 12.1c — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 2
// rpg jam: the second system — rules engine or mirrored AI; render pass.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>12.1c — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 2</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Day 2, grid edition: the risky sticky-note first, then exactly
    one system — a rules engine on the grid, a mirrored opponent,
    or the visual render. By the end of class, strangers could
    playtest what exists, and the jam is officially shippable-plus.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~50 min</span>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Order of the day (the <a href="10.1c.html">10.1c</a>
    day, tuned for grids):</strong></p>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Sticky note (15 min cap).</strong> Grid risks
            are predictable: pathfinding (DON'T — use straight
            lines and simple checks), turn-order bugs, or the
            renderer. Do the risky one now or cut it.</li>
        <li><strong>One system (20 min).</strong> Pick ONE:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>// (a) RULES: what a cell does when entered
if (grid[ny, nx] == WALL)  { /* blocked — don't move */ }
if (grid[ny, nx] == HAZZARD) { Lose(); }
if (grid[ny, nx] == EXIT)  { Win(); }

// (b) MIRRORED OPPONENT: the symmetry becomes a player 2/AI
if (grid[ay, ax] == grid[py, px] cell)  // same cell — who rules?
{
    // symmetric collision rule — heads up: DEFINE IT
}

// (c) RENDER: cells to sprites, index → world position
Instantiate(cellPrefab, new Vector3(col * 1f, row * -1f, 0), ...);</code></pre>
    <p>Note the (c) trap: row 0 is at the TOP of a text grid but
    your Unity y-axis points UP — the <code>* -1f</code> flips
    the board, and this one sign decides whether your game is
    readable or upside down.</p>

    <p><strong>Playtest smoke (10 min).</strong> Two minutes with
    a non-teammate. Watch them MOVE: do they understand the grid
    instantly, or fight it? Grid games are read in one glance or
    never.</p>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Day-2 grid traps</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Your rules fire on EVERY cell the player crosses —
                including cells they merely started on. When do
                you CHECK: at move start, at move end, or both —
                and which one does your design actually
                mean?</li>
            <li>The mirrored opponent moves the SAME frame you do
                — the classic symmetric deadlock. Whose input
                "happened first" when both press the same
                frame? (Define a tie rule — it is a DESIGN
                sentence, not a code accident.)</li>
            <li>The board renders but cells overlap/gap. What is
                your cellSize constant, and why does ONE
                number own every gap on screen?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Close of Day</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~5 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Shippable + wishlist</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Commit + push: <code>"12.1c: game (shippable)"</code>.
                Then the 10-minute-items wishlist for
                <a href="12.1d.html">12.1d</a> playtest
                polish.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Resolve the sticky risk inside the cap (or cut it, saying which)</li>
            <li>Show one rules/collision/render system complete</li>
            <li>Explain your symmetry's tie/deadlock rule in a sentence</li>
            <li>Declare shippable with the wishlist ranked</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-12/index.html">Unit 12: Season 2</a> — the jam map</li>
        <li><a href="12.1a.html">12.1a — Kickoff</a> — your pitch card</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="12.1d.html">12.1d — Grid/Symmetry Jam: Playtest</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="12.1c" title="12.1c Grid/Symmetry Jam: Build Day 2 — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
